sec.p:
We investigated the theoretical mechanism underlying the fluctuation of coupling weights Jee by analyzing their distribution in a quasi-steady state, assuming Jee,kj(t + Δt) ≈ Jee,kj(t) (Supplemental Text). Although a true static steady weight distribution does not exist in the desynchronized or bistable states due to the non-constant phases of each oscillator unit, the desynchronized condition allows for a quasi-steady state where the phase and weight distributions evolve on a sufficiently slow timescale, resulting in minimal fluctuation of the Jee distribution. Under this assumption, the coupling weights do not change as a result of the plastic modulation in Equations M11 and M14, leading to the following relationship (Supplemental Text, Equation S3).
(1)
Jee,kj=J^ee,kj+cexp⁡(−γ|Δθkj|)c=α(1−β)β \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{gathered} J_{e e, k j}=\hat{J}_{e e, k j}+c \exp \left(-\gamma\left|\Delta \theta_{k j}\right|\right) \\ c=\frac{\alpha(1-\beta)}{\beta} \end{gathered}$$\end{document}

Next, we considered the change in the phase difference for a pair of excUnits, Δθkj. Assuming that Jee,kj and Δθkj are constant, the following relationship can be derived from Equations 1 and M9 (Supplemental Text, Equation S18):
(2)
−Δω+Finh−Fexc(−kj)=−2{J^ee,kj+cexp⁡(−γ|Δθkj|)}sin⁡(Δθkj) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle -\Delta \omega+F_{i n h}-F_{e x c}^{(-k j)}=-2\left\{\hat{J}_{e e, k j}+c \exp \left(-\gamma\left|\Delta \theta_{k j}\right|\right)\right\} \sin \left(\Delta \theta_{k j}\right)$$\end{document}

The original Kuramoto model11,12,69 is expressed as:
(M1)
dθj(t)dt=ωj+KN∑kNsin⁡(θk(t)−θj(t)) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \frac{d \theta_{j}(t)}{d t}=\omega_{j}+\frac{K}{N} \sum_{k}^{N} \sin \left(\theta_{k}(t)-\theta_{j}(t)\right)$$\end{document}
where θj(t) is the phase of unit j at time t, ωj is the natural frequency of unit j following a probability distribution g(ω), N is the number of units, and K is the interaction strength among the oscillation units. In the original Kuramoto model, K is applied constantly and uniformly to all unit pairs. The order parameter R and the mean phase ψ are then calculated from the mean vector of the oscillators as:
(M2)
Reiψ=1N∑jNeiθj \begin{document}$$\displaystyle R e^{i \psi}=\frac{1}{N} \sum_{j}^{N} e^{i \theta_{j}}$$\end{document}
where i denotes an imaginary unit. Given Euler’s formula and Equation M2,
(M3)
1N∑kNsin⁡(θk−θj)=1N∑kNIm[eiθke−iθj]=Im[Reiψe−iθj]=Rsin⁡(ψ−θj) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \frac{1}{N} \sum_{k}^{N} \sin \left(\theta_{k}-\theta_{j}\right)=\frac{1}{N} \sum_{k}^{N} \operatorname{Im}\left[e^{i \theta_{k}} e^{-i \theta_{j}}\right]=\operatorname{Im}\left[R e^{i \psi} e^{-i \theta_{j}}\right]=R \sin \left(\psi-\theta_{j}\right) \quad$$\end{document}

Considering Equation M3, Equation M1 becomes
(M4)
dθjdt=ωj+KRsin⁡(ψ−θj) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d \theta_{j}}{d t}=\omega_{j}+K R \sin \left(\psi-\theta_{j}\right) \tag{EquationM4} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

In our previous EI-Kuramoto model14, the oscillation units were categorized into two groups: excitatory and inhibitory units, referred to as excUnits and inhUnits, respectively. Employing these terms clarified that the units in the model represented excitatory and inhibitory factors, distinguishing them from actual neurons or synapses. Zero-phase-lag sine interactions are purely attractive for excUnits and purely repulsive for inhUnits. Hence, the terms “excitatory” and “inhibitory” are used interchangeably with “attractive” and “repulsive,” respectively. The interaction strength K is divided into four quantifiable parameters: Kee (attractive strength between excUnits), Kii (repulsive strength between inhUnits), Kei (attractive strength from excUnits to inhUnits), and Kie (repulsive strength from inhUnits to excUnits). Kee, Kii, Kei, and Kie are distinct positive real numbers that are uniformly applicable across all the unit pairs. Consequently, Equation M4 becomes:
(M5)
dθj,excdt=ωj,exc+KeeRexcsin⁡(ψexc−θj,exc)−KieRinhsin⁡(ψinh−θj,exc) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \frac{d \theta_{j, e x c}}{d t}=\omega_{j, e x c}+K_{e e} R_{e x c} \sin \left(\psi_{e x c}-\theta_{j, e x c}\right)-K_{i e} R_{i n h} \sin \left(\psi_{i n h}-\theta_{j, e x c}\right)$$\end{document}
for excUnits, and
(M6)
dθj,inhdt=ωj,inh+KeiRexcsin⁡(ψexc−θj,inh)−KiiRinhsin⁡(ψinh−θj,inh) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{d \theta_{j, i n h}}{d t}=\omega_{j, i n h}+K_{e i} R_{e x c} \sin \left(\psi_{e x c}-\theta_{j, i n h}\right)-K_{i i} R_{i n h} \sin \left(\psi_{i n h}-\theta_{j, i n h}\right) \tag{EquationM6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
for inhUnits, where exc and inh represent the parameters for excUnits and inhUnits, respectively. The order parameter and mean phase of each excUnit and inhUnit are
(M7)
Rexceiψexc=1Nexc∑jNexceiθj,exc \begin{document}$$\displaystyle R_{e x c} e^{i \psi_{e x c}}=\frac{1}{N_{e x c}} \sum_{j}^{N_{e x c}} e^{i \theta_{j, e x c}}$$\end{document}
and
(M8)
Rinheiψinh=1Ninh∑jNinheiθj,inh \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} R_{i n h} e^{i \psi_{i n h}}=\frac{1}{N_{i n h}} \sum_{j}^{N_{i n h}} e^{i \theta_{j, i n h}} \tag{EquationM8} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

Given the rotational symmetry, when the mean natural frequencies of excUnits and inhUnits are equal, ω=ωexc=ωinh\begin{document}$\omega=\omega_{\text {exc }}=\omega_{\text {inh }}$\end{document}, they can be set to 0 rad/s, which is equivalent to rotating the coordinate frame by the mean natural frequency ω¯69\begin{document}$\bar{\omega}^{69}$\end{document}. For all simulations in this study: Nexc = 80, Ninh = 20; total simulation duration T = 50 s; time step dt = 0.01 s. The g(ω) followed the Gaussian distribution g(ω)∼𝒩(0, 0.01) for both excUnits and inhUnits. In Figs. 2–5, Kii and Kie are unified with the same parameter Ki to simplify the simulation.
(M10)
Ki=Kie=Kii \begin{document}$$\displaystyle K_{i}=K_{i e}=K_{i i}$$\end{document}

At each time step Δt (= dt = 0.01 s in this study), the Jee values were first modulated by Hebbian potentiation and then rescaled using homeostatic regularization rules. The Hebbian potentiation plasticity is implemented as an exponential function:
(M11)
JHebb=Jee,kj(t)+αexp⁡(−γ|Δθkj|) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle J_{\text {Hebb }}=J_{e e, k j}(t)+\alpha \exp \left(-\gamma\left|\Delta \theta_{k j}\right|\right)$$\end{document}
α is the learning rate for Hebbian plasticity. Note that α should scale proportionally with an inverse of Nexc as α ∝ 1/Nexc to maintain consistency across different system sizes. γ determines the sharpness of the exponential decay from the peak. Δθkj is the phase difference between excUnit k and j
(M12)
Δθkj=θk,exc−θj,exc(−π≤Δθkj<π) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \Delta \theta_{k j}=\theta_{k, e x c}-\theta_{j, e x c}\left(-\pi \leq \Delta \theta_{k j}<\pi\right)$$\end{document}

The implementation of Hebbian plasticity with a cosine function (Figure S1A) is defined as

(M13)

JHebb=Jee,kj(t)+α2{cos⁡(Δθkj)+1}
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} J_{\text {Hebb }}=J_{e e, k j}(t)+\frac{\alpha}{2}\left\{\cos \left(\Delta \theta_{k j}\right)+1\right\} \tag{EquationM13} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

The change in coupling weight at each time step ΔJee (Figure 3D and E) was defined as
(M15)
ΔJee=Jee(t+Δt)−Jee(t) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \Delta J_{e e}=J_{e e}(t+\Delta t)-J_{e e}(t)$$\end{document}
